#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097496} :activity/id #uuid "23266c50-a061-5ecc-b648-05f71a1b5ec6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619 :topic/id #uuid "0536fa23-cd17-50b6-b180-7b50962fb1ee" :topic/math-academy-id 1153} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097924 :tutorial/content "An **exponential function** is a function where a variable is contained within an exponent. For example, the function below is an exponential function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = 3^x\n$$\n\nJust like any other function, we can evaluate it for some particular value of $x$, say at\n\n$$\nx={\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\nby substituting this value of $x$ into the function:\n\n$$\nf(4) = 3^{\\color{blue}{4}} = 81\n$$\n\nAnother example of an exponential function is $g(x)$ below:\n\n$$\ng(x) = 16\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this function at\n\n$$\nx={\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\nusing the power of quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(4) &= 16 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{4} \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot \\frac{1^{4}}{2^{4}} \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExponential functions are of huge practical importance in science, engineering, economics, and many other disciplines. Let's practice evaluating them." :tutorial/id #uuid "155b9a63-2c55-5f26-a41f-b777e7afa911" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8843 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "28a95194-58c2-5aab-b5ec-1edc7f9c4953" :step/math-academy-id 166109 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097497}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096657 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096390 :question/id #uuid "d03736b3-a7d8-52b3-894d-4ce2d04ee31c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14257"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9283940a-cad8-51b5-b8c2-dc42ad8de264" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048683}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097323}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Exponential Function"} :step/id #uuid "e368b5da-f79a-5928-9c93-43f4a0dee7a5" :step/math-academy-id 166110 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097498}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096658 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096391 :question/id #uuid "a83c1301-18cf-5622-8a4a-73f9d18e36b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14260"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bab04f2-5e9a-55e7-9669-63e15938be0a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097243}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function in a Modeling Problem"} :step/id #uuid "103957a3-310c-54ce-8227-8f216b6f4683" :step/math-academy-id 166114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097499}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097925 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're given the following exponential function:\n\n$$\nf(t) = C\\cdot 4^t\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that\n\n$$\nf(3) = 128\n$$\n\nCan we use this information to find the unknown constant $C$?\n\nTo solve this problem, we substitute $t=3$ and\n\n$$\nf(t) = 128\n$$\n\nand solve for $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n128 &= C \\cdot 4^{3} \\\\\n128 &= C \\cdot 64 \\\\\n\\frac{128}{64} &= C \\\\\n2 &= C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C=2$. So our function expressed in full is\n\n$$\nf(t) = 2\\cdot 4^t\n$$\n\nWe sometimes say that the **initial value** of the function is $2$, because if we evaluate $f$ at $t=0$, we get\n\n$$\nf(0) = 2\\cdot 4^0 = 2\\cdot 1 = 2\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6820cdab-7e0a-5a90-adb1-880e4eb4a8de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8846 :tutorial/title "Solving for an Unknown Constant"} :step/id #uuid "0d53b42c-c931-5e58-b073-7217b0bb9ac4" :step/math-academy-id 166118 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096659 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096392 :question/id #uuid "ca8cd4d6-8136-59bb-a033-6ba9ae8c06ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-14262"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cee2b0-6cc8-5281-8ea2-02400b2980e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097322}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Initial Value"} :step/id #uuid "546da0c9-4c8f-57c2-9e29-200dc3ee9f2d" :step/math-academy-id 166119 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097501}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097926 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following exponential function:\n\n$$\nh(x) = 2^x\n$$\n\nCan we find the value of $x$ that gives $h(x)=64$? Indeed, we can. To do this, we'll need to use our knowledge of exponential equations.\n\nFirst, we use the fact that $h(x)=64$ to write our equation as\n\n$$\n2^x = 64\n$$\n\nThis is an exponential equation. Since we know that $64$ is a power of $2$, we can solve this equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{x} &= 64 \\\\\n2^{x} &= 2^{6} \\\\\nx &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "94964a23-690b-5cea-8e8c-5946840cca5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8848 :tutorial/title "Solving for an Unknown Input Value"} :step/id #uuid "2934358a-1be2-5bac-84d5-feb8432e9804" :step/math-academy-id 166122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096660 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096393 :question/id #uuid "02cbb721-a880-5600-8093-fc768ae5e993" :question/math-academy-id "e-14264"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a21f047-5100-5f8b-9448-a5c2044e4e65" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050511}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097198}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Input Value"} :step/id #uuid "499e92bc-8919-5fbf-8915-9df0c0cca3d7" :step/math-academy-id 166123}] :activity/title "Exponential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096918}]}
